> Make that range small, say expert jumping distance.  Make it fall
> slowly for a bit, then more rapidly.  Do you teleport to 99% or 98%?
> What if where you really want to get is 95%?

It all depends on how much it matters.  When it matters, people will
stick to 100% range.  When it doesn't matter, it doesn't matter.  Most
of the time it will matter; given the suggestion as written, there's 
little point in teleporting four squares UNLESS you're using it to 
bypass a specific situation and/or monster that you don't want to fight.
Beyond that, if you could end up back at the downstairs by using 
teleport, players simply won't use it.

I'm not against people being able to spend tele scrolls and/or Pw 
by jumping past monsters -- gives some purpose back to teleport
control.  There's only one situation where it's a problem, and that's
being able to get to the upstairs itself once you're in sight.

> I think you're right that there are a lot of good players who are
> sufficiently risk-averse that they'd do exactly that, at least in the
> overwhelming majority of cases.  There'd still be the temptation every
> now and then to take the risk, though 

Good players are good because they don't yield to temptation to take
risks that are not overwhelmingly in their favor.  When designing things
like this, it is mandatory to assume a perfect player.

>> That works out to players mostly not using teleport except as an
>> escape mechanism, though; not really where I want to go since I'm
>> wanting to reintroduce teleport control as a more meaningful
>> intrinsic post-Amulet.
> Not if you combine it with the probability curve for control blocking.
> Then you can teleport shorter distances with (reasonable) reliability
> and preserve an Asteroids-style "I need to be gone *now*" teleport.
> (Or, as discussed, people could drop the amulet for a safe jump in two
> turns.)

That's beside the point though, as in emergencies people will not be
interested in a random teleport if any other option is available, and 
as such will either drop-and-go in two turns, or use what's available 
in the "100% controlled" range if that will do the trick.  If there 
is no 100% controlled range, then they'll only use it in emergencies 
to escape; not really creating what I'd like, since then I would've just
added back random teleport, not controlled teleport.

>> This is what I'm blocking -- the player needs to have to walk back up
>> through Gehennom, rather than just traverse a few no-teleport levels.
> I suspect there's a sweet spot where you can do a function as above
> and have teleport your way out of Gehennom be a gambit rather than an
> obviously correct tactic.

Shortening the range, even at 100% control, almost totally removes the 
possibility of teleporting out of Gehennom.  You simply don't find 
enough teleport scrolls, and you don't regenerate Pw fast enough.  In
vanilla, you typically ran out of teleport scrolls after 10 or so
levels, but that was still plenty to let your Pw take you the rest of
the way.

There is the "pet quantum mechanic, conflict, and no MC" solution, but
it rapidly becomes much more difficult as well due to the shortened
range and need to keep magic-whistling him over to you.

> Perhaps capping the probability of successful controlled teleport with
> the amulet somewhere in the 80-90% range, 

